    "body": "## Problem\n\nOpening a window with `enter=false` while in a buffer with a buffer-local cwd will change the global cwd to the buffer-local cwd.\n\nI discovered this while I had some floating windows opening in the new directory listing and my fuzzy picker cwd was unexpectedly changing.\n\n## Steps to reproduce\n\nsuppose you have some directory structure like this:\n```\ndir1/\n├── fileA\n└── dir2/\n    └── fileB\n```\nthen run:\n\n1) `cd dir1 && nvim --clean .`\n2) `:pwd` -> observe `dir1/` is cwd\n3) `<CR>` on `dir2/` (`dir2/` is now buffer cwd)\n4) open a window with `enter = false`, e.g.\n     - `:lua vim.api.nvim_open_win(vim.api.nvim_create_buf(false, true), false, { relative = 'editor', height = 5, width = 10, row = 0, col = 99})`\n     - `:lua vim.api.nvim_open_win(vim.api.nvim_create_buf(false, true), false, { split = 'right' })`\n5) `<CR>` on `fileB`\n6) `:verbose pwd` -> observe `[global] dir1/dir2/` is now cwd\n\n\n## Expected behavior\n\nOpening a window (enter or not) while in the directory listing should not change global cwd. \nConcretely, I mean if you repeat the steps above, step `6)` should report `[global] dir1`, the same as if you had skipped `4)`\n\nI'm not sure what the final consensus is regarding #41210, but it is certainly **unexpected** behavior (in my opinion) that `enter=true` results in global cwd being maintained, while `enter=false` will change it. Even more so when you consider that one may want to implement something like floating preview window of the file under the cursor in the directory listing, which I would not expect to cause the global cwd to change.\n\nNotes:\n- pressing `K` on a filename with a typical `vim.notify` plugin will show a popup `man.lua: no manual entry for <file>` will subsequently cause the global cwd to change to wherever that file is... which is very unintuitive behavior.\n- I tested both float and split with `enter=true` and global cwd is maintained after jumping to file\n\n## System info\n\n- Nvim version (nvim -v): `v0.13.0-dev-1322 ga458bfb595` neovim/neovim@a458bfb595\n- Vim (not Nvim) behaves the same?: \n- Operating system/version: Linux 7.1.8-100.fc43.x86_64\n- Terminal name/version: kitty\n- $TERM environment variable: `xterm-kitty`\n- Installation: built from source\n\n",
